From 8094ef7c2b47d4ab99be2ef83292b363c7d67652 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Muhammad Hassaan Shaukat <mr.hassaan50@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 05:16:40 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] Add two offline analyses that test the carrier question
 without a model call

coalition_advantage_mean answers whether a pair still gains once text is deleted. It does
not answer what Track 3.1 actually asks, which is whether the actions themselves carry
anything an outsider can read. These two harnesses read the artifacts of runs that already
happened, make no calls, and cost nothing.

decode_actions.py ranks the six candidate pairs of a 4-agent run from the claim trace only
-- the share of measured joint-reward gain a pair captured, and how far its members' own
claims compete for the same skill tags -- with the true coalition hidden from the decoder.
The null is exact at 1/6, so no permutation sampling is needed. Results are split by
whether messaging was available, because only the blocked arms bear on the carrier claim.

An earlier version of the ranking code excluded the pair being scored from its own candidate
set, so the rank collapsed to a constant and the true pair appeared to finish last in 12 of
12 artifacts. That looked like a striking negative result and was a bug; the rewrite scores
every pair against the full set and the label-blind composite now ranks the true pair first
in 4 of 7.

counterfactual_replay.py substitutes single actions -- drop a claim, swap two claims, replace
one action with the exact solver's choice -- and rescores through the environment's own reward
formula, reused from compute_jira_optimal.py. It refuses an arm unless replaying the actual
assignment reproduces the logged joint_reward within 0.01, and reports every rejection instead
of dropping it silently, so the counterfactuals inherit the scorer's faithfulness rather than
assuming it. All seven arms pass the gate to the third decimal.

Both keep only the newest artifact per arm: a rerun of the same arm is not an independent sample.
